To implement a text editor that supports multiple list types (like code blocks and paragraphs) using Angular, you'll need to combine some existing components from the Angular Material library or other libraries like `ngx-editor` for rich text editing. Below is an example of how you might structure such an application.

### Step 1: Install Required Packages

First, ensure you have Angular installed. Then install any necessary packages:

```bash
npm install @angular/material ngx-rich-text-editor --save
```

### Step 2: Create Components

Create two main components: one for the text editor itself and another for handling the multi-list functionality.

#### Text Editor Component (`text-editor.component.html`)

```html
<mat-toolbar>
    <button mat-icon-button color="primary" (click)="toggleEditMode()">
        <mat-icon>edit</mat-icon>
    </button>

    <h3>{{ mode === 'code' ? 'Code Block' : 'Paragraphs' }}</h3>
</mat-toolbar>

<div [ngClass]="mode">
    <textarea [(ngModel)]="content" placeholder="Type your content here..."></textarea>
</div>

<button mat-raised-button color="primary" (click)="addList()">Add List</button>
<button mat-raised-button color="secondary" (click)="removeSelectedList()">Remove Selected List</button>
```

#### Multi-List Handling Component (`multilist.component.html`)

This component will handle the selection and manipulation of lists within the editor.

```html
<select [(ngModel)]="selectedList" (change)="handleListChange(selectedList)">
    <option value="">None</option>
    <!-- Options for different list types -->
    <option *ngFor="let list of ['unordered', 'ordered']" value="{{list}}">{{list}}</option>
</select>
```

### Step 3: Implement Logic in the Main Component (`app.component.ts`)

In your main application component, initialize both components and manage their state.

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { MatToolbarModule } from '@angular/material/toolbar';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-root',
    templateUrl: './app.component.html',
    styleUrls: ['./app.component.css'],
    imports: [
        MatToolbarModule,
        // Add more modules as needed
    ]
})
export class AppComponent {
    mode = 'code'; // Default mode
    selectedList = ''; // Placeholder for list type selection
    content = '';

    toggleEditMode() {
        this.mode = this.mode === 'code' ? 'paragraphs' : 'code';
    }

    addList() {
        if (this.selectedList && this.content.includes('\n')) {
            const newLineIndex = this.content.indexOf('\n');
            const newList = `${newLineIndex > -1 ? '\n\n' : ''}${this.content.slice(newLineIndex)}\n${this.selectedList}`;
            this.content = newList;
        }
    }

    removeSelectedList() {
        this.content = this.content.replace(this.selectedList + '\n', '');
    }

    handleListChange(listType) {
        this.selectedList = listType;
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Run Your Application

Make sure to run your Angular application. The setup should look something like this:

```bash
ng serve
```

Navigate to http://localhost:4200 in your browser to see your application running.

### Summary

This setup uses Angular's material toolbar for navigation between modes and provides basic multi-list support via a dropdown menu. Adjustments can be made based on specific requirements, such as adding additional list styles or refining user interactions.

